{
  "dom_id": "step-q80669",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-1$ and $x=4.$",
